SM(176,{"n":"1 Peter 2","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/1 Peter 2.mp3","d":2397.6,"s":[[0.32,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Council of God."],[9.04,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in biblical studies."],[12.56,"And it's an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[22.08,"Chapter 2. We have yet another, therefore."],[29.44,"Therefore, because this is true."],[32.32,"That this gospel was preached to you, that this is the nature of this life in this world and corruptible things in this world."],[38.72,"Therefore, laying aside all malice, all deceit, hypocrisy, envy, and all evil speaking, as newborn babes desire the pure milk of the word, capital W, that you may grow thereby, if indeed you have tasted that the Lord is gracious."],[58.24,"Right. So if you have this new birth he was talking about."],[66.48,"Then we're like newborn babies."],[68.88,"Right? And so he's making this comparison."],[71.28,"Newborn babies. Uh, don't do a lot of hypocrisy."],[77.68,"Don't do a lot of evil speaking."],[81.84,"Um. These are all things that newborn babies don't do yet."],[87.36,"They figure it out pretty quick, but you're truly a newborn."],[91.44,"If you just came out of the womb, you're not you're not about any of this."],[95.12,"You're mostly crying and what?"],[97.68,"Wanting to be fed."],[99.6,"Right? And that's why he says you need to be fed."],[104.72,"Right. By Christ, by coming to know Christ."],[109.68,"Right. Verse 4."],[115.36,"Coming to him as to a living stone, rejected indeed by men, but chosen by God and precious."],[126.6,"You also, as living stones, are being built up a spiritual house, a holy priesthood, to offer up spiritual sacrifices acceptable to God through Jesus Christ."],[139.88,"I'm going to read a little more because this is all part of one analogy."],[144.28,"Therefore, it is also contained in the scripture behold, I lay in Zion a chief cornerstone, elect precious, and he who believes on him will by no means be put to shame."],[156.04,"Therefore, to you who believe, or to you who are faithful, he is precious, but to those who are disobedient, the stone which the builders rejected is become the chief cornerstone, and a stone of stumbling in a rock of offense."],[171.64,"They stumble being disobedient to the word, to which they were also appointed."],[178.6,"Okay, so a few things here."],[184.2,"So he's making this analogy, right, of the Christian community, the church, right, being like a building."],[196.52,"With Christ as the chief cornerstone."],[204.2,"This is true all through the ancient Near East, and even into the Greek and Roman periods, the Phoenicians did this, there were very elaborate ceremonies, religious rituals, for laying the cornerstone of a building."],[220.12,"Some of them got very dark in the pagan world."],[224.88,"Some of them included like sacrificing infants, putting infants in the walls, really disturbing stuff."],[232.88,"Not all of them."],[234.32,"That's sort of the extreme, but a lot of them did not involve human sacrifice."],[240.0,"But they usually involved some kind of sacrifice."],[242.16,"Right? Sacrifices of animals."],[244.56,"The anointing of the stone."],[246.0,"There were elaborate sort of religious rituals for this."],[250.24,"So it's not just a question of as we think about modern construction."],[256.08,"Oh, we're going to lay the cornerstone and have a little ceremony and get the mayor to cut the ribbon and you know, whatever."],[261.28,"Right. This is this was a much bigger thing."],[264.72,"So the idea of Christ being laid as the cornerstone."],[268.16,"The cornerstone was literally seen as sacred and holy."],[272.72,"Right. And its sacredness and holiness is what blessed the whole building, the whole construction process and the whole building once it was completed and made it sacred and holy, if you're building a temple, which is what is being described here."],[286.88,"Right. So that's a key element there that might not pop out at us."],[291.2,"But so Christ is this is the cornerstone, and then we are living stones who are put together into this, into this temple to God."],[301.2,"Uh, so that, and then, and then St."],[304.0,"Peter mixes his metaphors."],[306.32,"So we're being built into this spiritual house, a holy priesthood."],[309.92,"So we're also the priests who serve at the house."],[313.6,"And uh and in the house that we are, but that we're also in his priests, we're offering these these sacrifices that are acceptable to God through Jesus Christ."],[325.8,"Right? So what we offer, we offer, right, to God through Christ who makes it acceptable."],[336.36,"More on that in a second."],[339.96,"So then he cites, or he quotes another uh text from Isaiah."],[348.76,"Behold, I lay in Zion a chief cornerstone, elect precious, he who believes on him, he who is faithful to him, will by no means be put to shame."],[358.2,"Right? So he says this is referring to Christ."],[361.88,"Because if you go and look and read in the context of Isaiah, I won't take us back there now to read Isaiah 28."],[367.72,"But he's talking about the redemption of Israel coming."],[372.2,"Right? The end of the exile, the redemption of Israel, and that begins with him laying this cornerstone."],[381.32,"So that begins with Christ, St."],[383.56,"Peter is saying."],[385.16,"And so then the process of building the rest of this temple with us in it is the unfolding of the salvation of Israel."],[393.64,"Sorry, dispensationals. Right."],[396.36,"Um then notice in verse 7, this is another thing in the translation."],[402.44,"Notice that here it's contrasts in this translation, the New King James, to you who believe versus the disobedient."],[413.4,"Does that make sense as a contrast?"],[417.8,"There are people who believe something is true, and there are people who behave disobediently."],[424.84,"Or does it make more sense to say to those who are faithful versus those who are disobedient?"],[430.76,"Because those are opposites."],[433.72,"Right? That's a better translation of the word that's being used here for believe anyway."],[438.64,"Right? So this is another place where that's superabundantly clear."],[442.64,"That that's what it's getting at."],[444.64,"Right. So for us, he's the cornerstone."],[449.28,"Right? That makes the whole building holy and begins our redemption."],[453.12,"But for those who are disobedient to him, then the stone which the builders rejected, that rejection."],[462.32,"Talking about the rejection of Christ."],[464.88,"Right? Remember, Christ's coming is the beginning of the redemption of Israel."],[468.48,"But a lot of people who would have considered themselves part of Israel rejected him."],[473.76,"Not everybody by any means, but a good portion."],[478.32,"Rejected him. But he's become the chief cornerstone nonetheless."],[483.44,"And then the second part, a stone of stumbling and a rock of offense."],[488.96,"That's from Psalm 118 and Isaiah 8, respectively, or Psalm 117 in the Greek."],[495.84,"And as he says that stone of stumbling and a rock of offense, they stumble being disobedient to the word, capital W."],[504.48,"They're being disobedient to Christ, who is that cornerstone."],[510.0,"To which they also were appointed."],[513.68,"So I get to say sorry, Calvinists, because those people who rejected Christ, right, were also appointed to Christ, according to St."],[522.72,"Peter here. They rejected Christ, even though Christ was their Messiah, their savior, the beginning of their salvation, too, they rejected him."],[541.64,"Right? So the difference was them."],[544.2,"They were disobedient rather than faithful."],[546.36,"The difference was not Christ or the way Christ related to them."],[550.2,"The distant difference is whether they were faithful or disobedient."],[555.64,"Verse 9, turning now back to the Christian community, but you are a chosen generation, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, his own special people, that you may proclaim the praises of him who called you out of darkness into his marvelous light."],[581.4,"So now we get more sorry, dispensationalists."],[584.84,"So he is here, St."],[586.44,"Peter is here citing from Exodus what God says in making his covenant with Israel."],[595.0,"Yahweh, the God of Israel says, You are to be a kingdom of priests."],[599.16,"That's where the royal priesthood comes from."],[603.0,"Right? A holy nation."],[604.92,"That's why he was constituting them."],[607.8,"St. Peter is saying this to the Christian church."],[613.72,"To Christian communities."],[616.28,"He's using the Israel language."],[619.56,"Right. And that holy nation is Aios Ethnos."],[626.12,"A holy ethnos."],[629.64,"Christians are an ethnos."],[632.04,"Right. Um, which is where we get the word ethnicity."],[638.36,"Right? And generation is yenos."],[643.24,"Root of genealogy."],[648.56,"He's saying all these things, all this language that the Old Testament applies to Israel, St."],[653.92,"Peter is here directly applying to Christian churches as the fulfillment of that, as he's arguing here that the Christian churches there scattered around in Asia Minor, people from all different backgrounds, right, are the fulfillment of the redemption of Israel that Isaiah prophesied."],[675.84,"So dispensationalism don't work here."],[681.52,"And if you want to argue, as someone might, well, maybe he's just talking to Jewish Christians."],[689.76,"If they're still trying to do that thing."],[693.28,"They're still trying to do that, then unfortunately we come to verse 10."],[699.76,"Who once were not a people, but are now the people of God, who had not obtained mercy, but now have obtained mercy."],[712.16,"So not only is that a problem if you want to say he's talking to Jewish Christians, and that's why he can apply the Israel language."],[720.48,"So that's obviously a problem because he's saying you weren't a people and now you are."],[726.16,"So he's clearly talking to the church as a newly constituted people."],[731.68,"Being the fulfillment of the redemption of Israel, but he's citing language from the prophet Hosea."],[743.2,"Uh Prophet Hosea famously was told to uh as a prophet, the prophets not only said things, but they did prophetic actions."],[752.92,"Right? He would have God would have them go do something to demonstrate or display something."],[758.04,"And Hosea was told to go and marry a prostitute."],[762.6,"And this was to show to Israel what God's marriage with Israel was like."],[771.0,"Was the idea."],[772.2,"Right? That he had wed himself to someone who was unfaithful."],[776.04,"Right. And then after Hosea went and married a prostitute, they had children together, and God told him what to name his children, not because, oh, we're going to give your kids weird names, but because the kids were taken to the temple to be named publicly."],[795.32,"And one of those kids was named Loami, which in Hebrew means not my people."],[806.12,"So in Hosea, this is the language St."],[810.44,"Peter's drawing on here, God says to Israel at that point, you are not my people."],[817.72,"That he's going to make for himself a new people who previously were not a people."],[841.76,"Is enough to demonstrate that in the site of 1 Peter, whoever you think wrote it, if you don't believe it was St."],[850.0,"Peter, it's still in the Bible."],[853.2,"That this apostolic author saw the church not as a new Israel and not as replacing Israel, but as Israel."],[863.68,"As the continuation of Israel, as a renewed Israel."],[868.4,"Right. Verse 11, beloved, I beg you, as sojourners and pilgrims, abstain from fleshly lusts, which war against the soul."],[885.36,"So what's the metaphor here?"],[889.76,"Sojourners and pilgrims."],[891.44,"Remember, he just used this Passover metaphor."],[896.08,"Right? So you've been set free from slavery."],[900.56,"Right? You weren't a people, now you've been made a people."],[903.6,"Where did that happen?"],[904.56,"Mount Sinai. Right."],[907.68,"Now we're out here in the wilderness."],[910.16,"Right? We're out of the wilderness."],[913.2,"And so you're picking up stakes, you're traveling in a camp, you're not permanently settled."],[920.48,"And if you read during that time in the Torah, God said to Israel again and again."],[926.88,"Right now out here in the desert, you're dependent on me."],[930.72,"They're dependent on God out there."],[933.6,"Right? For food, for clothing to provide for water."],[938.08,"Right? He said, but the time's gonna come when you come into the land and you plant your fields and you grow your crops, you're gonna forget about me."],[946.72,"You're gonna fall into sin."],[948.72,"Right? It's gonna happen."],[950.24,"Right, God knew it was gonna happen."],[953.2,"And it did happen."],[954.8,"But so at the time he said, you need to not fully settle down."],[959.2,"This is one of the reasons why they had what's called the Feast of Booths or the Feast of Tabernacles, Sukkot."],[965.0,"That they would all sort of pick up stakes and live in booths and tents the way they lived in the wilderness again."],[971.88,"To remind them."],[973.72,"This is not actually our permanent home."],[976.84,"Right here in this world."],[980.12,"And there's even a group in Jeremiah, there's this small clan from one of the tribes who never settled because of this."],[988.68,"They remained living nomadically in the land that God gave to their clan."],[993.32,"They just never put down roots there."],[996.76,"Right? It's sort of a semi-nomadic, semi-monastic way of living, right, to reflect this."],[1002.92,"And they get commended, Jeremiah, like these are the last righteous people left, you know, as they're going into exile."],[1009.64,"Um but so is taking that and applying it now to us in the Christian community."],[1016.12,"Right? This world isn't our home."],[1018.6,"Right? None of the nations of this world are our home and our real citizenship."],[1022.92,"Right? We're pilgrims on our way through."],[1025.88,"Right? God has an inheritance waiting for us, right, at the end, when Christ is revealed."],[1033.96,"Right? Until then, we're roaming around, we're wanderers, we're pilgrims."],[1039.64,"So he says, Because this is how you are, we need to abstain from fleshly lusts which war against the soul."],[1046.44,"Right? All those fleshly desires are what?"],[1049.56,"Things of this world."],[1051.08,"Right? Don't get attached."],[1055.56,"Verse 12, having your conduct honorable among the Gentiles."],[1061.4,"Who are Gentiles here, after what we just read?"],[1065.28,"People outside the church."],[1068.56,"Yeah. So remember that marking off at Passover."],[1076.16,"After that night, anybody who was marked with the blood was an Israelite, and anyone who wasn't wasn't."],[1084.32,"St. Peter's drawing that analogy to the blood of Christ."],[1089.28,"So now that Christ has come, anybody who's marked out with the blood of Christ is part of God's people."],[1096.4,"Israel, the church."],[1098.48,"Anybody who isn't, isn't it?"],[1101.6,"Doesn't matter who they're descended from."],[1105.84,"And so they're still the nations."],[1108.16,"They're the 70 nations out there."],[1110.48,"Right. But so having your conduct honorable among the Gentiles, that when they speak against you as evildoers, they may by your good works, which they observe, glorify God in the day of visitation."],[1124.96,"So this is part of that sort of persecution they've faced."],[1131.76,"Right? There are people making accusations against them, slanderous accusations."],[1136.56,"And so St."],[1137.28,"Peter is saying, because there are people making these slanderous accusations against you."],[1147.6,"You need to make sure none of those accusations are true."],[1150.48,"That they're all slanderous."],[1157.12,"When God visits his people, that's not like grandpa showing up for cookies and tea."],[1162.24,"That's right. When God's visitation is more like wait till your dad gets home."],[1167.36,"It's there's gonna be some things settled."],[1169.36,"There's gonna be some reckoning that's gonna happen, right, when he comes to visit."],[1173.08,"And so keeping that in mind."],[1176.28,"Make sure that all those accusations are false."],[1179.24,"And live before them in a way that makes that apparent."],[1183.88,"Verse 13, therefore submit yourselves to every ordinance of man for the Lord's sake."],[1189.4,"Okay, so he's saying, therefore."],[1192.76,"Meaning, what do I mean by that?"],[1194.52,"Right? Having your conduct be right above reproach."],[1199.64,"Submit to every ordinance of man for the Lord's sake."],[1203.64,"Right? Lots of laws, lots of unjust laws."],[1207.4,"We're talking about the Roman Empire, okay?"],[1209.72,"Right? But he's saying, hey, obey the law."],[1214.12,"Right? Obey the law."],[1215.8,"Until you're commanded to sin, obviously."],[1220.68,"But until then, you obey the law, even though it's not your law, even though you're not a Roman citizen."],[1227.48,"You're a sojourner."],[1230.28,"Whether to the king is supreme, or to governors, as to those who are sent by him for the punishment of evildoers and for the praise of those who do good."],[1244.92,"Okay. Now there's an interesting thing here, because he says, to the king is supreme."],[1251.88,"This may be a little bit of a dig."],[1256.2,"Because uh Caesar was not a king."],[1259.08,"Rex is the one title he never took."],[1262.44,"And wouldn't. Right?"],[1264.2,"Because the kings in Roman history, the tyrannists."],[1268.76,"The word tyrant, right, were looked back at, oh, those were the dark ages before the republic."],[1274.08,"Right? We don't go back to that."],[1274.84,"Imperator basically means conqueror."],[1275.76,"So Caesar never took the title king."],[1278.56,"Right? That's where we get emperor."],[1282.8,"Right? So he would never be going."],[1284.56,"So if you're referring to him as the king, there's a little bit, a little bit of a political commentary there."],[1291.12,"That he's maybe more like those tyrants that he wants to admit."],[1295.36,"But he's saying that you need to obey the laws, even though they're made by kind of a tyrant."],[1303.2,"Or to governors."],[1304.48,"As to those who are sent."],[1305.68,"So why? Right?"],[1308.08,"Because what he's saying is it's the hymn is not the king."],[1313.36,"They didn't capitalize it here."],[1315.2,"It should be capitalized."],[1318.24,"Right? So they're not sent by the king, but by God."],[1322.24,"By God. The king and the governors are sent by God."],[1327.2,"Right? This is sort of parallel to what St."],[1328.96,"Paul says in Romans 13."],[1330.64,"They're sent by God to punish evildoers and praise those who do good."],[1336.08,"What is that?"],[1336.72,"To judge rightly."],[1338.08,"To establish justice."],[1340.0,"Right. So the civil authorities are responsible before God."],[1346.4,"Right. We talked about this with David in particular."],[1349.68,"To image God in this world by establishing justice."],[1355.52,"God's justice. Right?"],[1357.44,"They're ministers of God to establish justice in the world."],[1361.6,"And they're going to be held accountable by God for how they do that."],[1366.24,"Whether they do that righteously or wickedly."],[1369.44,"Whether they take bribes, whether they write oppress the weak."],[1376.24,"And so he's saying, these are people who are sent by God."],[1379.84,"And this is a theme not just in Romans 13."],[1382.2,"I mean, this is what Christ says to Pilate."],[1385.16,"You'd have no power if it wasn't given to you from above."],[1387.72,"He's not talking about Caesar."],[1389.96,"Right. Um. And so this is what they're to do with it."],[1394.84,"And so you."],[1398.36,"You follow the law."],[1400.12,"Verse 15, for this is the will of God that by doing good you may put to silence the ignorance of foolish men."],[1407.8,"Right? So you go out there and do good."],[1410.68,"And so when people make these accusations against you, other people will be like, what are you talking about?"],[1415.88,"This person, you know, they help the poor, they help the sick, they do all these things in the community."],[1421.32,"Why are you coming and making these bizarre accusations?"],[1425.08,"Right. Verse 16, as free, yet not using liberty as a cloak for vice, but as bondservants of God."],[1433.8,"So remember the manumission thing."],[1435.72,"We've been set free from slavery to sin."],[1438.44,"Right? So we are free now."],[1440.84,"Right? But that doesn't mean we could use our liberty as a cloak for vice."],[1444.12,"Right? I'm free now, so now I can go do whatever I want."],[1447.88,"That's not right."],[1449.96,"So he says, but as bondservants to God, as slaves to God."],[1454.12,"We're now free from sin to serve God."],[1459.8,"Verse 17, honor all people, love the brotherhood."],[1464.36,"So that's honor everybody."],[1466.52,"Venerate everybody. Give everyone honor and respect."],[1472.44,"Love the brotherhood."],[1473.8,"Right. Remember, brethren, that's within the Christian community."],[1477.0,"Your brothers and sisters in Christ, love them."],[1480.28,"Fear God. Right."],[1484.04,"I mean, have awe and reverence toward God and honor the king."],[1488.64,"Now you notice the word honor there is the same word he used for honor all people."],[1495.36,"So he says, treat Caesar, who he calls king again, like everybody else."],[1500.24,"Right? Give him honor and respect."],[1503.28,"Right? Verse 18."],[1511.2,"Servants be submissive to your masters with all fear, not only to the good and gentle, but also to the harsh."],[1518.08,"So let's not be euphemistic."],[1520.24,"He's talking about slaves."],[1522.8,"He's talking about Roman slaves."],[1524.72,"Because there are a lot of Christians who are Roman slaves at this time."],[1529.28,"Right? And this is in keeping with what he just said."],[1533.36,"Right? And the undercurrent of this, remember, is that if this is written in the 50s, like the late 50s, early 60s."],[1543.68,"We got another Jewish revolt brewing that's going to result in the destruction of the temple."],[1550.88,"Okay. We've got these zealot groups, we've got this, and this isn't going to be the first issue."],[1556.96,"This is just going to be one, and even that's not going to be the end."],[1560.0,"We're going to have the barkok for rebellion that's going to get Jerusalem wiped out."],[1563.28,"But so there's this strong undercurrent within a lot of Jewish communities, different Jewish sects, that say, hey, violence toward the Romans is divine violence."],[1577.2,"Right. That that's what that's what the Messiah is going to be all about."],[1582.0,"He's going to be wreaking vengeance on these Romans."],[1586.96,"And so a big undercurrent here of what St."],[1591.08,"Peter's, what we've already read and what we're now reading is Christians aren't to be a part of that."],[1598.04,"Right. That's not how Christians approach this."],[1603.88,"Not because we think Caesar is great and wonderful."],[1607.96,"That's why we keep calling him king."],[1610.12,"Right. Right? But because God is the one, as he said, who's going to hold them accountable for what they do with their power."],[1620.6,"We don't do that through violence."],[1622.92,"God does that."],[1624.6,"Right? And that's really what we want, by the way."],[1629.08,"Right? Because we also want it to be God who deals with us according to our sins."],[1637.56,"I don't want everyone I've ever wronged in my life coming back for revenge."],[1641.88,"Especially violent revenge."],[1644.44,"Right. I want the forgiveness of God."],[1648.04,"I want God to judge between us."],[1650.44,"Right. And so if we want our sins to be left to God, then we need to live the leave the sins of others to God."],[1661.64,"This is talking about slaves."],[1662.68,"He's talking about how slaves need to be in."],[1664.28,"Because they're, you know, to an extent, anyone who wasn't a Roman citizen was effectively Caesar's slave."],[1672.2,"Right. In the Roman Empire."],[1674.84,"You could at any point be functionally enslaved by any Roman citizen who came by."],[1682.28,"And if they're living in these Roman colonies that St."],[1684.84,"Peter named. But obviously, if you're a literal slave, even more so."],[1692.76,"Right? Even more so."],[1696.16,"And so he's saying, likewise."],[1698.48,"No slave revolts, no Christian slave revolts revolts."],[1702.32,"Don't go and try and kill your master."],[1704.4,"Right. And he's saying, not just the good and gentle, but also the harsh."],[1711.44,"Meaning, it's not your job to judge him."],[1714.56,"God is going to judge him."],[1717.52,"Right? That's not letting him off the hook."],[1721.44,"Right? That may end up being much worse."],[1724.72,"Right. Than just you knocking him out and escaping or something."],[1729.44,"His judgment may be far more extreme in the end."],[1733.68,"Verse 19, for this is commendable, if because of conscience toward God one endures grief, suffering wrongfully."],[1742.88,"Right. So he's saying, if you have a good conscience toward God, and so you don't seek vengeance, you don't take the path of violence."],[1755.2,"And because of that, you endure grief and suffering wrongfully."],[1759.76,"That you do not deserve."],[1761.92,"Right? Then this is commendable."],[1765.44,"Now he's not just saying commendable, like, good job, gold star."],[1768.72,"Right? Commendable is in there's a reward for this at the revelation of Christ."],[1775.6,"Right? It's God who's going to commend you for this, not just St."],[1779.44,"Peter giving you an ataboy."],[1782.56,"Verse 20, for what credit is it if when you are beaten for your faults, you take it patiently."],[1789.68,"But when you do good and suffer, if you take it patiently, this is commendable before God."],[1796.08,"So what he's saying is, if you suffer and you deserve it."],[1803.4,"Because you brought it about."],[1805.88,"Through your own bad behavior."],[1808.04,"That's not commendable."],[1809.16,"There's not a reward for that."],[1811.72,"Right. And you're lonely."],[1817.96,"Then you being lonely and abandoned is your own fault."],[1820.36,"That's not commendable."],[1821.8,"Right? But if you have a clean conscience toward God and you're trying to do good, and everyone abandons you."],[1828.2,"Then there's a reward waiting for you."],[1837.48,"Verse 21, for to this you are called, because Christ also suffered for us, leaving us an example that you should follow his steps, who committed no sin, nor was deceit found in his mouth."],[1853.32,"So the example he gives is of a slave being beaten for essentially no reason."],[1859.72,"Which happened a lot."],[1862.76,"Right, just out of pure sadism, if nothing else, right, in the Roman world."],[1867.24,"Okay. Well, when he compares that to Christ, that's a pretty literal comparison."],[1874.68,"Christ was literally beaten."],[1877.96,"And as he points out, did nothing."],[1881.48,"Committed no sin ever."],[1884.28,"Right? But he was beaten."],[1887.4,"And verse 23, when he was reviled, did not revile in return."],[1894.04,"Right? When people spoke ill about him and slandered him and said horrible things about him and spit it, he didn't pay it back."],[1902.2,"He did not threaten, but committed himself to him who judges righteously."],[1909.84,"Right? He gave it to God to sort out."],[1912.88,"And then, by the way, prayed that his father would forgive them."],[1917.36,"Right? But he gave it to him."],[1919.44,"He did not avenge himself."],[1922.08,"Who himself bore our sins in his own body on the tree."],[1926.72,"That we having died to sins might live for righteousness by whose stripes you were healed."],[1936.56,"So this language of bore his our sins in his body."],[1944.48,"Right. What the language is literally saying is that our sins, as if they were things."],[1953.12,"This kind of ontological thing, were in his body when he hung on the cross."],[1960.24,"So what is this an analogy to?"],[1964.32,"Peter's been working with the Torah."],[1966.8,"Okay. Day of Atonement."],[1969.44,"There's two goats."],[1972.88,"Right? This is a comparison to one of those goats."],[1977.6,"The goat for Azazel, they called it."],[1981.36,"Scapegoat. That goat they would take."],[1984.56,"They didn't kill that goat, they didn't sacrifice that goat."],[1988.0,"But the high priest would come and save the sins of the people and put them on the goat."],[1996.08,"Right. And the goat, then would bear those sins, would carry those sins out into the wilderness."],[2009.36,"Take them away."],[2010.68,"Take them away."],[2012.92,"Right? So they're now gone."],[2019.08,"Right? And the people are now purified of them because they're gone."],[2024.92,"And now when it says that we haven't died to our sins."],[2034.44,"So when Christ dies, he takes those sins where Hades."],[2043.96,"Right? Why was it called the goat for Azazel?"],[2048.28,"Azazel is the demonic spirit out there in the wilderness."],[2052.28,"Right? He's the one who inspired those sins in the first place."],[2056.68,"So the goat takes them back to him."],[2059.0,"Right? Return to sender."],[2060.68,"Here's all the sin."],[2061.72,"Right? Take it back."],[2063.24,"Right? Christ is the same thing."],[2066.84,"St. Peter is saying."],[2068.36,"Right? He takes our sin, he takes him to Hades, devil's down there, return to sender."],[2076.28,"Right. And so if we've been baptized, we've died with Christ, so we've died to our sins, as St."],[2082.52,"Paul says, and St."],[2083.4,"Peter is using the same language, so that we can now live for righteousness."],[2091.96,"What's he just been talking about?"],[2094.04,"He was comparing the beating and mocking that a slave might take from a wicked master to the beating and mocking that Christ took from wicked men."],[2112.44,"What rose up by the time of St."],[2114.52,"Peter, as we know from a bunch of sources, both Jewish and Christian, is that when that goat was driven out into the wilderness."],[2124.16,"This is described in detail, for example, the epistle of Barnabas."],[2127.52,"Um that goat would be spit at hit with reeds and sticks."],[2138.4,"They'd throw things at it, they'd mock it."],[2141.44,"And that was a way of enacting doing that to their sin."],[2145.92,"It wasn't that they just wanted to destroy this goat's self-esteem."],[2149.76,"The idea was we're rejecting our sin."],[2154.96,"Right? We want nothing to do with it."],[2159.04,"Through this sort of ritual act."],[2162.96,"Right. And so when St."],[2164.88,"Peter says, by his stripes we are healed, he's not saying that we deserved a beating, but Christ took it instead of us."],[2174.4,"Right? That doesn't fit with the context, by the way, very clearly."],[2178.8,"Because he's talking about us potentially a slave taking a beating he does not deserve."],[2186.4,"Right. So that doesn't make any sense."],[2192.48,"Right. The goat also really kind of took a beating."],[2194.72,"The goat didn't do anything wrong."],[2196.88,"They cast lots to determine which goat was which."],[2204.4,"So I don't know."],[2205.2,"I might want to go live my life in the wilderness, even if people were mean to me before I did it."],[2209.2,"Anyway, that's um."],[2213.36,"So, right, and it's not that the the goat deserved it."],[2218.0,"But it's it's that rejection."],[2219.6,"And that death of Christ."],[2225.16,"And this is really clear in St."],[2226.84,"Matthew's gospel, because St."],[2228.52,"Matthew's gospel literally uses all the language about that to describe Christ's beating."],[2235.8,"The people spitting at him, the people striking with the reed, it's the same word for reed on the head."],[2242.04,"That's what they did to the goat."],[2243.56,"It's all the stuff they did to the goat in St."],[2245.32,"Matthew's gospel they do to Christ."],[2248.36,"Right. And so the idea there is not to, as a lot of modern people think, put the blame for Christ's death on the Jews."],[2256.92,"It's the exact opposite."],[2259.08,"Right? It's that they meant it maliciously toward Christ, but in reality, what he was doing was taking their sin and taking it away."],[2270.36,"Right. So that's what that's what St."],[2273.24,"Peter's getting at here in context."],[2275.88,"It's another metaphor."],[2277.32,"So we're seeing he's he's drawing these extended metaphors out of the Torah, is really what he's doing here all through this epistle."],[2288.52,"Verse 25, for you were like sheep going astray."],[2293.56,"Right. So sheep, if you leave them alone and don't pay attention, they're gonna wander off all over the place."],[2302.12,"That was you."],[2302.68,"You were wandering off all over the place."],[2305.48,"But have now returned to the shepherd and overseer of your souls."],[2314.2,"And um that uh that word overseer there is uh episcopos, the word that we translate is bishop, but that word was used not coincidentally, uh, it's used to translate a Hebrew word with very much the same meaning, somebody who watches over."],[2336.8,"There was a word for a herdsman, so shepherd and herdsman."],[2341.6,"Right, sort of being being put together here."],[2344.4,"The one who watches over your souls."],[2353.28,"So this I think is a good place, time-wise, and everything, and we're at a chapter break to stop for the evening."],[2360.48,"We'll pick up in chapter three next time."],[2364.8,"It is not impossible that we finish first Peter next time, but I don't know."],[2369.68,"We'll see what happens."],[2371.2,"Um, but thank you, everybody."],[2375.2,"Listen next time as Father Stephen De Young continues his study of the scriptures on the whole council of God."],[2382.56,"Father Stephen's email address is whole counsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2387.92,"That's whole counsel at ancient faith.com."]],"o":[{"t":22.08,"lv":2,"h":"1 Peter 2:1-3 - Newborn babes","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Chapter <b>2</b> opens with \"<b>therefore</b>\" - because the <b>gospel was preached to you</b></li><li class=\"d1\">because of the nature of <b>this life</b> and <b>corruptible things</b> in this world</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Lay aside</b> malice, deceit, hypocrisy, envy, evil speaking</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>newborn babes</b> desire the pure <b>milk of the word</b> (capital W) that you may grow</li><li class=\"d1\">\"if indeed you have <b>tasted</b> that the Lord is gracious\"</li><li class=\"d0\">If you have the <b>new birth</b> he was talking about → you're like <b>newborn babies</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Newborns don't do <b>hypocrisy</b>, <b>evil speaking</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"they figure it out pretty quick\" - but a true newborn is not about any of this</li><li class=\"d1\">mostly <b>crying</b> and <b>wanting to be fed</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So you need to be <b>fed by Christ</b>, by <b>coming to know Christ</b></li></ul>"},{"t":109.68,"lv":2,"h":"2:4-8 - Living stones, one analogy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verse 4</b>: coming to him as a <b>living stone</b>, rejected by men, chosen by God and <b>precious</b></li><li class=\"d1\">you also, as <b>living stones</b>, built up a <b>spiritual house</b>, <b>holy priesthood</b></li><li class=\"d1\">offer <b>spiritual sacrifices</b> acceptable to God through Jesus Christ</li><li class=\"d0\">Reads further because \"it's all part of <b>one analogy</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Isaiah: \"I lay in Zion a <b>chief cornerstone</b>, elect, precious\"</li><li class=\"d1\">believer \"by no means put to shame\"</li><li class=\"d1\">to you who believe he is <b>precious</b>; to the disobedient the <b>rejected stone</b> became the chief cornerstone</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>stone of stumbling</b>, <b>rock of offense</b> - they stumble being disobedient to the word</li><li class=\"d0\">Analogy: <b>Christian community</b>, the church, like a <b>building</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Christ</b> = chief cornerstone</li></ul>"},{"t":178.6,"lv":3,"h":"Cornerstone rituals","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Cornerstone laying true all through the <b>ancient Near East</b>, into <b>Greek and Roman</b> periods</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Phoenicians</b> did this - very elaborate <b>religious rituals</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Some got \"very dark\" in the pagan world</li><li class=\"d1\">some included <b>sacrificing infants</b>, putting them in the walls - \"really disturbing\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not all of them\" - extreme end</li><li class=\"d1\">many did not involve human sacrifice, but usually <b>some sacrifice</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>animals</b>, <b>anointing of the stone</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not like modern construction: ceremony, mayor, <b>ribbon</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"a much bigger thing\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Cornerstone literally seen as <b>sacred and holy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">its holiness <b>blessed the whole building</b> and construction process</li><li class=\"d1\">made it sacred if building a <b>temple</b> - which is what's described here</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Might not pop out at us\"</li></ul>"},{"t":291.2,"lv":3,"h":"Living stones and priests","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Christ</b> = cornerstone; <b>we</b> = living stones put together into a <b>temple to God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Peter \"<b>mixes his metaphors</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">built into a <b>spiritual house</b>, a <b>holy priesthood</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we're the <b>priests who serve at the house</b> - and the house itself</li><li class=\"d1\">priests offering <b>sacrifices acceptable to God through Jesus Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">What we offer, we offer <b>to God through Christ</b>, who makes it acceptable</li><li class=\"d1\">\"more on that in a second\"</li></ul>"},{"t":339.96,"lv":3,"h":"Isaiah 28 - redemption of Israel","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Quotes <b>Isaiah</b>: \"I lay in Zion a chief cornerstone, elect, precious\"</li><li class=\"d1\">he who is <b>faithful</b> to him \"by no means put to shame\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Peter says this refers to <b>Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">in context of <b>Isaiah 28</b>: the coming <b>redemption of Israel</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>end of the exile</b> - begins with <b>laying this cornerstone</b></li><li class=\"d1\">begins with <b>Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Building the rest of the temple, <b>us in it</b> = the <b>unfolding of the salvation of Israel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"sorry, <b>dispensationals</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":396.36,"lv":2,"h":"2:7-8 - Faithful vs disobedient","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verse 7</b>: NKJV contrasts \"you who <b>believe</b>\" vs the disobedient</li><li class=\"d1\">does that contrast make sense? people who <b>believe something is true</b> vs people who <b>behave disobediently</b></li><li class=\"d1\">better: <b>faithful</b> vs <b>disobedient</b> - \"those are <b>opposites</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">better translation of the word for <i>believe</i> - \"superabundantly clear\" here</li><li class=\"d0\">For us: he's the <b>cornerstone</b> - makes the whole building <b>holy</b>, begins our <b>redemption</b></li><li class=\"d0\">For the <b>disobedient</b>: the stone the builders <b>rejected</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>rejection of Christ</b> - his coming = start of Israel's redemption</li><li class=\"d1\">many who \"considered themselves part of Israel\" rejected him - \"not everybody by any means, but a good portion\"</li><li class=\"d1\">but he became the <b>chief cornerstone</b> nonetheless</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Stone of stumbling, rock of offense</b>: <b>Psalm 118</b> and <b>Isaiah 8</b> (Psalm <b>117</b> in the Greek)</li><li class=\"d0\">They stumble, disobedient to the word = <b>Christ</b>, the cornerstone</li><li class=\"d0\">\"To which they also were <b>appointed</b>\" - \"sorry, <b>Calvinists</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">those who rejected Christ were <b>appointed to Christ</b>, per Peter</li><li class=\"d1\">he was <b>their Messiah</b>, beginning of <b>their</b> salvation too</li><li class=\"d1\">difference was <b>them</b>: <b>faithful vs disobedient</b>, not Christ or how he related to them</li></ul>"},{"t":555.64,"lv":2,"h":"2:9 - Chosen generation, royal priesthood","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verse 9</b>: turning back to the <b>Christian community</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>chosen generation, royal priesthood, holy nation</b>, his own special people</li><li class=\"d1\">that you may <b>proclaim the praises</b> of him who called you <b>out of darkness into his marvelous light</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Sorry, dispensationalists\" - Peter <b>citing Exodus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God making covenant: \"you are to be a <b>kingdom of priests</b>\" → source of <b>royal priesthood</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>holy nation</b> - how he was constituting them</li><li class=\"d0\">Peter says it to <b>Christian communities</b> - using the <b>Israel language</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Holy nation</b> = <i>ethnos hagios</i>, a holy <i>ethnos</i></li><li class=\"d1\">Christians are an <i>ethnos</i> → <b>ethnicity</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Generation</b> = <i>genos</i> → root of <b>genealogy</b></li><li class=\"d0\">All the OT language for Israel applied <b>directly to Christian churches</b> as the <b>fulfillment</b></li><li class=\"d1\">churches scattered in <b>Asia Minor</b>, people of <b>all backgrounds</b></li><li class=\"d1\">fulfillment of the <b>redemption of Israel</b> Isaiah prophesied</li><li class=\"d0\">→ \"dispensationalism don't work here\"</li></ul>"},{"t":693.28,"lv":3,"h":"2:10 - Not a people, now God's people","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Objection: maybe he's just writing to <b>Jewish Christians</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">\"unfortunately we come to <b>verse 10</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verse 10</b>: who once were <b>not a people</b>, now the <b>people of God</b>; had not obtained mercy, now have</li><li class=\"d1\">problem for the Jewish-Christians reading: \"you weren't a people and now you are\"</li><li class=\"d1\">the church = a <b>newly constituted people</b>, fulfillment of Israel's redemption</li><li class=\"d0\">Language from the prophet <b>Hosea</b></li><li class=\"d1\">prophets didn't only speak - they did <b>prophetic actions</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Hosea told to <b>marry a prostitute</b> - to show <b>God's marriage with Israel</b>: wed to someone <b>unfaithful</b></li><li class=\"d1\">children named publicly at the <b>temple</b>, names given by God</li><li class=\"d1\">one named <b>Lo-ammi</b> = <b>\"not my people\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God to Israel: \"you are not my people\" - he'll make a <b>new people</b> who previously weren't a people</li><li class=\"d0\">Enough to show: the apostolic author of <b>1 Peter</b> (\"whoever you think wrote it\") saw the church not as <b>new Israel</b> or <b>replacing Israel</b>, but <b>as Israel</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>continuation</b> of Israel, a <b>renewed Israel</b></li></ul>"},{"t":868.4,"lv":2,"h":"2:11 - Sojourners and pilgrims","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verse 11</b>: beloved, as <b>sojourners and pilgrims</b>, abstain from <b>fleshly lusts</b> which war against the soul</li><li class=\"d0\">Metaphor: he just used the <b>Passover</b> image</li><li class=\"d1\">set free from slavery → made a people at <b>Mount Sinai</b> → now in the <b>wilderness</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>picking up stakes</b>, traveling in a camp, not permanently settled</li><li class=\"d0\">Torah: out in the desert <b>dependent on God</b> - food, clothing, water</li><li class=\"d1\">coming time: plant fields, grow crops, \"<b>forget about me</b>\", fall into sin - \"it did happen\"</li><li class=\"d1\">so: don't <b>fully settle down</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Feast of Booths</b> / Tabernacles = <i>Sukkot</i></li><li class=\"d1\">live in <b>booths and tents</b> again → \"not actually our <b>permanent home</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Group in <b>Jeremiah</b>: small clan who <b>never settled</b>, lived nomadically</li><li class=\"d1\">\"semi-nomadic, semi-monastic\"; <b>commended</b> as the last righteous people going into exile</li><li class=\"d0\">Applied to us: <b>this world isn't our home</b>; no nation is our real <b>citizenship</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>pilgrims</b>, wanderers; <b>inheritance waiting</b> when Christ is revealed</li><li class=\"d0\">→ abstain from fleshly lusts = things of this world - \"<b>don't get attached</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1055.56,"lv":2,"h":"2:12 - Conduct among the Gentiles","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verse 12</b>: conduct <b>honorable among the Gentiles</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Gentiles</b> here = <b>people outside the church</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Passover marking: after that night, anyone <b>marked with the blood</b> = Israelite, anyone not wasn't</li><li class=\"d1\">Peter draws analogy to the <b>blood of Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">marked with Christ's blood = <b>God's people</b>, Israel, the church</li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't matter who they're <b>descended from</b>; others are still the <b>nations</b>, the <b>70 nations</b></li><li class=\"d0\">When they speak against you as <b>evildoers</b>, they glorify God by your <b>good works</b> in the <b>day of visitation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">context: <b>persecution</b>, <b>slanderous accusations</b></li><li class=\"d0\">→ make sure <b>none of the accusations are true</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>God's visitation</b> - \"not grandpa showing up for cookies and tea\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"more like <b>wait till your dad gets home</b>\" → a <b>reckoning</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Live so it's <b>apparent</b> the accusations are false</li></ul>"},{"t":1183.88,"lv":2,"h":"2:13-14 - Submit to every ordinance","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verse 13</b>: \"therefore\" = conduct <b>above reproach</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>submit to every ordinance of man</b> for the Lord's sake</li><li class=\"d1\">\"lots of unjust laws\" - the <b>Roman Empire</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>obey the law</b> - until commanded to <b>sin</b>, obviously</li><li class=\"d1\">even though it's not your law, you're not a Roman citizen - \"you're a <b>sojourner</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verse 14</b>: to the king as supreme, or <b>governors</b> sent by him for <b>punishment of evildoers</b> and <b>praise of those who do good</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1244.92,"lv":3,"h":"\"The king\" - a dig at Caesar?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"To the king as supreme\" - may be \"a little bit of a <b>dig</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Caesar was <b>not a king</b>; <i>Rex</i> = \"the one title he <b>never took</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">kings in Roman history = <b>tyrants</b> (<i>tyrannos</i>) - \"the dark ages before the <b>republic</b>\", \"we don't go back to that\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><i>Imperator</i> basically = <b>conqueror</b> → <b>emperor</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Calling him king = \"a little bit of <b>political commentary</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">maybe more like <b>those tyrants</b> than he wants to admit</li><li class=\"d0\">Still: <b>obey the laws</b>, even from \"kind of a tyrant\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1303.2,"lv":3,"h":"Sent by God to establish justice","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Or to governors, as to those who are <b>sent</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not sent by the king but by <b>God</b> (\"Him\" should be capitalized)</li><li class=\"d1\">king and governors are <b>sent by God</b> - parallel to <b>Romans 13</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Sent to punish evildoers, praise the good = <b>judge rightly</b>, <b>establish justice</b></li><li class=\"d1\">civil authorities <b>responsible before God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">as with <b>David</b>: to <b>image God</b> in this world by establishing <b>God's justice</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>ministers of God</b>, held accountable for how they do it</li><li class=\"d1\">righteously or wickedly - <b>bribes</b>, <b>oppressing the weak</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Theme not just in Romans 13: <b>Christ to Pilate</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"you'd have no power if it wasn't <b>given from above</b>\" - not talking about Caesar</li><li class=\"d0\">→ \"you follow the law\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1400.12,"lv":2,"h":"2:15-17 - Doing good, free yet slaves","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verse 15</b>: will of God - by <b>doing good</b> put to silence the <b>ignorance of foolish men</b></li><li class=\"d1\">accusers confronted: \"they help the <b>poor</b>, the <b>sick</b>... why these <b>bizarre accusations</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verse 16</b>: <b>free</b>, yet not using liberty as a <b>cloak for vice</b>, but as <b>bondservants of God</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>manumission</b> - set free from <b>slavery to sin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not \"I'm free now, I can do whatever I want\" - \"that's not right\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>slaves to God</b> = free from sin <b>to serve God</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verse 17</b>: <b>honor all people</b>, love the brotherhood</li><li class=\"d1\">honor = <b>venerate</b> everybody; give <b>honor and respect</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>brotherhood</b> = brethren within the <b>Christian community</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>fear God</b> = awe and reverence</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Honor the king</b> - same word as \"honor all people\"</li><li class=\"d0\">→ treat <b>Caesar</b> (again \"king\") like everybody else</li></ul>"},{"t":1503.28,"lv":2,"h":"2:18 - Servants and masters","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verse 18</b>: servants submissive to masters <b>with all fear</b>, not only the good and gentle but also the <b>harsh</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"let's not be euphemistic\" - <b>Roman slaves</b></li><li class=\"d1\">many Christians were <b>Roman slaves</b> at the time</li><li class=\"d1\">in keeping with what he just said</li><li class=\"d0\">Undercurrent: written late <b>50s, early 60s</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Jewish revolt</b> brewing → destruction of the <b>temple</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>zealot</b> groups; not the first issue, not the last - <b>Bar Kokhba</b> rebellion wipes out Jerusalem</li><li class=\"d1\">strong undercurrent in Jewish sects: <b>violence toward Romans = divine violence</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>Messiah</b> would be \"wreaking <b>vengeance</b> on these Romans\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Big undercurrent of Peter: <b>Christians aren't part of that</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1603.88,"lv":3,"h":"Not violence - God judges","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not because \"Caesar is great and wonderful\" - \"that's why we keep calling him <b>king</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">But because <b>God</b> will hold them <b>accountable</b> for their power</li><li class=\"d1\">\"we don't do that through violence. <b>God does that</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">and that's what we want, too</li><li class=\"d0\">We want <b>God</b> to deal with us according to our sins</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I don't want everyone I've ever wronged coming back for <b>revenge</b>\", especially violent</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I want the <b>forgiveness of God</b>\"; want God to judge between us</li><li class=\"d0\">→ want our sins left to God → <b>leave the sins of others to God</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1661.64,"lv":3,"h":"Slaves, citizens, no revolts","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Talking about <b>slaves</b></li><li class=\"d1\">anyone not a <b>Roman citizen</b> = effectively <b>Caesar's slave</b></li><li class=\"d1\">could be <b>functionally enslaved</b> by any Roman citizen at any point</li><li class=\"d1\">applies to those in the <b>Roman colonies</b> Peter named; a literal slave \"<b>even more so</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Likewise\": <b>no Christian slave revolts</b></li><li class=\"d1\">don't try to <b>kill your master</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Not just the good and gentle, but also the <b>harsh</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not your job to <b>judge</b> him - <b>God</b> will</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not letting him off the hook\" - may be <b>much worse</b> than you knocking him out and escaping</li><li class=\"d1\">\"his judgment may be far more <b>extreme</b> in the end\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1733.68,"lv":2,"h":"2:19-20 - Commendable suffering","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verse 19</b>: commendable if because of <b>conscience toward God</b> one endures grief, <b>suffering wrongfully</b></li><li class=\"d1\">good conscience → no <b>vengeance</b>, no <b>violence</b></li><li class=\"d1\">endure suffering you <b>don't deserve</b> → <b>commendable</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Commendable\" ≠ \"good job, gold star\"</li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>reward</b> at the <b>revelation of Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>God</b> commends you, not Peter giving an \"<b>ataboy</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verse 20</b>: what credit if beaten for your <b>faults</b> and take it patiently?</li><li class=\"d1\">suffer what you <b>deserve</b>, brought on by your <b>bad behavior</b> → not commendable, no reward</li><li class=\"d1\">lonely and abandoned through <b>your own fault</b> → not commendable</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>clean conscience</b>, trying to do good, everyone abandons you → <b>reward waiting</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1837.48,"lv":2,"h":"2:21-23 - Christ's example","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verse 21</b>: to this you are <b>called</b>; Christ <b>suffered for us</b>, leaving an <b>example</b>, follow his steps</li><li class=\"d1\">committed <b>no sin</b>, no <b>deceit</b> in his mouth</li><li class=\"d0\">His example: a <b>slave beaten for essentially no reason</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"happened a lot\" - <b>pure sadism</b> in the Roman world</li><li class=\"d0\">Compared to Christ: \"a pretty <b>literal comparison</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ <b>literally beaten</b>, did nothing, <b>no sin ever</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verse 23</b>: when <b>reviled</b>, did not revile in return</li><li class=\"d1\">slandered, spit on - \"he didn't <b>pay it back</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">did not threaten; <b>committed himself to him who judges righteously</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"gave it to <b>God to sort out</b>\"; prayed the Father would <b>forgive them</b></li><li class=\"d1\">did not <b>avenge himself</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1919.44,"lv":3,"h":"2:24 - Bore our sins: the goat for Azazel","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Who himself <b>bore our sins in his own body on the tree</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">that we having <b>died to sins</b> might <b>live for righteousness</b>; by whose <b>stripes</b> you were healed</li><li class=\"d0\">Language literally: our sins, \"as if they were things\" - \"<b>ontological</b>\" - <b>in his body</b> on the cross</li><li class=\"d0\">Analogy to what? Peter working with the <b>Torah</b> - <b>Day of Atonement</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>two goats</b>; Christ compared to one: the <b>goat for Azazel</b>, the <b>scapegoat</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not killed, not sacrificed; <b>high priest</b> put the people's sins on it</li><li class=\"d1\">goat <b>bears</b> and <b>carries</b> the sins out to the <b>wilderness</b> → <b>take them away</b>, people <b>purified</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Azazel</b> = demonic spirit in the wilderness - \"the one who <b>inspired those sins</b> in the first place\"</li><li class=\"d1\">goat takes them back to him: \"<b>return to sender</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ the same: takes our sin to <b>Hades</b>, \"devil's down there, return to sender\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Baptized → <b>died with Christ</b> → died to our sins (as <b>Paul</b> says) → live for righteousness</li></ul>"},{"t":2091.96,"lv":3,"h":"The goat spat on - not blame on the Jews","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">What was he just talking about? <b>beating and mocking</b> of a slave by a wicked master = Christ's from wicked men</li><li class=\"d0\">By Peter's time (<b>Jewish and Christian</b> sources): the goat <b>driven into the wilderness</b> was <b>spit at</b>, hit with <b>reeds and sticks</b></li><li class=\"d1\">described in the <b>Epistle of Barnabas</b>; thrown things, mocked</li><li class=\"d1\">a way of <b>enacting rejection of their sin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"not to destroy the goat's <b>self-esteem</b>\" - \"we want <b>nothing to do</b> with it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"By his stripes we are healed\" ≠ we deserved a beating, Christ took it instead</li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't fit the context - a slave taking a beating he <b>does not deserve</b></li><li class=\"d1\">goat didn't do anything wrong - <b>lots cast</b> for which goat was which</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I might want to go live my life in the wilderness, even if people were mean to me\"</li><li class=\"d1\">it's the <b>rejection</b> and <b>death of Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Matthew's gospel</b> uses all that goat language</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>spitting</b>, striking with the <b>reed</b> on the head - same word</li><li class=\"d0\">Point is <b>not</b> to blame <b>the Jews</b> for Christ's death - \"the <b>exact opposite</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">they meant it <b>maliciously</b>, but he was <b>taking their sin away</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Peter drawing <b>extended metaphors out of the Torah</b> all through the epistle</li></ul>"},{"t":2288.52,"lv":2,"h":"2:25 - Shepherd and overseer","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Verse 25</b>: you were like <b>sheep going astray</b></li><li class=\"d1\">leave sheep alone, they <b>wander off all over the place</b> - \"that was you\"</li><li class=\"d1\">now <b>returned</b> to the <b>shepherd and overseer of your souls</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Overseer</b> = <i>episkopos</i> - the word we translate <b>bishop</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"not coincidentally\" - translates a <b>Hebrew word</b> with the same meaning: one who <b>watches over</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrew word for <b>herdsman</b> - <b>shepherd and herdsman</b> put together</li><li class=\"d1\">the one who <b>watches over your souls</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2353.28,"lv":2,"h":"Stopping point","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Good place to stop - <b>time</b> and a <b>chapter break</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Pick up in <b>chapter 3</b> next time</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Not impossible that we <b>finish 1 Peter</b> next time, but I don't know\"</li></ul>"}],"next":"Next time: <b>1 Peter 3</b> - may finish <b>1 Peter</b>, \"we'll see\""});
